<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1943 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">With Marshal Tito</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sh">Uz maršala Tita</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of Yugoslavia's wartime Partisans, written in 1943 in praise of their leader, Josip Broz Tito.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Vladimir Nazor</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Oskar Danon</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Yugoslav Partisans and Socialist Yugoslavia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbo-Croatian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Yugoslavia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1943</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Vladimir Nazor, music by Oskar Danon, 1943)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Uz maršala Tita&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uz-marsala-tita">With Marshal Tito</a>), first called &quot;Pjesma o pesti&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> about the Fist), is a Yugoslav Partisan anthem in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>, leader of the country's liberation movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Vladimir Nazor wrote the Serbo-Croatian words and Oskar Danon the music, in 1943, when relations between Tito and the Soviet leadership were close.</p><p>Its original opening named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> together with Stalin. According to Danon, at its first hearing, at the second session of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-council-for-the-national-liberation-of-yugoslavia">AVNOJ</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> in 1943, where Tito was named Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, the audience spontaneously sang the line with Tito alone. After the Tito–Stalin split of 1948 the original version was no longer performed or printed in songbooks.</p><p>A middle verse answers the ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a>, whose wartime Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> played down the Slavic origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and claimed they descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>; the song rejects that theory and affirms the Slavic origin of all South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. It was translated into all the national languages of Socialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and into minority languages such as Slovak. In the late 1970s, shortly before his death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, where a ceremonial choir sang it in Serbo-Croatian to honour him.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Partisan song in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> and of the unity of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>; martial and celebratory.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words.</p></section>
</div>
